# `M154c` / `C6`, `C7` — the positive half of `expect request fails` / `expect request connects`.
#
# Runs under `env unreachableHost` (port 9, the universally-unassigned discard port), the dedicated
# env `tests/.env-specific/unreachable-host.tflw` established and whose blast-radius fence this
# respects rather than working around with an absolute URL. Its sibling
# `request-fails-live-control.tflw` runs the *same two matchers* under `env local` against a host
# that is very much up, and the pair is the claim — see that file for why one alone proves nothing.
#
#   npx tflw run --env unreachableHost tests/.constructs/request-fails-unreachable.tflw

@constructs @requestlifecycle
test "a closed port is a connection-layer failure, and `connects` refuses it"
  api GET /health
  expect request fails matching "fetch failed"

@constructs @requestlifecycle
test "the same request, asserted the other way round — this test MUST end red"
  # `connects` and `fails` are complements. Asserting only that `fails` passes here would be
  # satisfied by a matcher that passes unconditionally; this line is what rules that out.
  api GET /health
  expect request connects
